{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794063147,"nonce":"F5CF6gHPnH0CiiLwps7uBimx6qc=","title":"Mesh rendering cost scales with mesh count: skeleton demos run at 27-41 fps on Pixel 10 Pro","message":"Measured 2026-11-07 on a Pixel 10 Pro (Chrome 156, worker on WebGL2), worker requestAnimationFrame rate over 6 s: DamagedHelmet (1 mesh) 82 fps; strangler 41 fps (worst gap 67 ms); buster_drone (39 meshes) 27 fps (worst gap 83 ms). The drone's render closure saw dt 26-83 ms, median 40. Desktop Chrome on WebGPU is a steady 16.7 ms per frame with one render call per frame. The demos advance animation by wall-clock dt, so animation time is correct; the cost is frame time, which shows as stepping on fast rotors (authored at about 30 keyframes per second).\n\nLikely cause, not yet profiled: every mesh builds its own command encoder and render pass over the full-size Rgba16Float HDR target (load and store each time) and submits separately, plus a joint-matrix texture upload per skinned mesh and a separate shadow pass per caster. Tile-based mobile GPUs pay for each extra pass. Candidate fix: one HDR pass per frame with all opaque draws, one for blended draws, one shadow pass for all casters, and joint matrices packed into a single upload.\n\nNot a cap: the web runner has no fps limit; animation_fps_cap in the demos is only read by the native windowed runner.\n\nRelated: 7c5bebf (mobile 3D failures).","files":null}]}